<section class="text-base-content">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pt-12 pb-20 md:px-10 md:pt-20 md:pb-28">
    <div class="grid gap-6 border-b border-base-300 pb-10 md:grid-cols-12 md:items-end">
      <div class="md:col-span-8">
        <p class="tag text-primary">The story</p>
        <h1 class="mt-5 text-[clamp(3rem,7vw,7rem)] leading-[0.98] tracking-[-0.015em] text-balance">A dyer's house, <em>restored</em></h1>
      </div>
      <p class="text-lg text-base-content/80 md:col-span-4">Calle del Telar 14, from the Vásquez dyers to the five rooms of today.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="mt-10">
      <div class="aspect-[4/3] overflow-hidden rounded-box sm:aspect-[16/9] md:aspect-[21/9]" data-settle data-sun>
        <img src="/media/basins.jpg" class="size-full object-cover">
      </div>
      <figcaption class="script mt-3 text-lg text-base-content/75">The last of the dyers' basins, a fountain now, in the middle of the courtyard.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="mt-14 grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-16">
      <div class="prose-linen text-lg">
        <p>In 1871 the Vásquez family built a house around a courtyard at the edge of Jalatlaco, a barrio of tanners and weavers, and set four stone basins in the middle of it. For sixty years they boiled cochineal for its reds and indigo for its blues, and sold their skeins to the weavers of the valley.</p>
        <p>In 1932 Aurelio Ramos rented the front room and hung out a tailor's sign. When he retired in 1979 the doors closed, and the house stood empty through forty rainy seasons, until a fig tree came up through the roof of the back room.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-8">
        <blockquote class="border-l-[1.5px] border-dashed border-primary pl-6 text-[clamp(1.75rem,2.6vw,2.4rem)] leading-snug text-balance"><p class="display">“The first time we opened the gate, the basins were full of rainwater and still pink at the bottom. We knew then what to call the rooms.”</p><footer class="tag mt-4 text-base-content/70">Inés Bautista</footer></blockquote>
        <div class="prose-linen text-lg">
          <p>Inés and Theo bought the house in 2018 and spent three years restoring it with masons from the barrio, in adobe, lime and the old beams. The first guests came in November 2021, for the Day of the Dead.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
